{{smaller block|<poem>To themselves ''appropriating''
The Spirit of God.</poem>{{right|''Milton'', P. L., xii. 518.}}}}
{{smaller block|A man is a knave who falsely, but in the panic of turning all suspicion from himself, charges you or me with having ''appropriated'' another man’s jewel.{{right|''De Quincey'', Secret Societies, ii.}}}}
{{smaller block|The estate I so admired and envied is my own. It is the nature of the soul to ''appropriate'' all things.{{right|''Emerson'', Compensation.}}}}
'''2.''' In general, to take for any use; put to use.
{{smaller block|In solar light the leaves of plants decompose both carbonic anhydride and water, ''appropriating'' the carbon and the hydrogen of each for their own growth and nutrition.{{right|''W. A. Miller'', Elem. of Chem., § 351.}}}}
'''3.''' To set apart for or assign to a particular purpose or use, in exclusion of all other purposes or uses: as, Congress ''appropriated'' more money than was needed; to ''appropriate'' a spot of ground for a garden.
{{smaller block|The profits of that establishment [the Post-office] had been ''appropriated'' by Parliament to the Duke of York.{{right|''Macaulay'', Hist. Eng., iii.}}}}
'''4.''' In ''eccles. law'', to annex, as a benefice, to an ecclesiastical corporation, for its perpetual use.

'''appropriate''' (a̤-prō′pri-ạ̄t), ''a.'' and ''n.'' [< LL. ''appropriatus'', pp.: see the verb.] '''I.''' ''a.'' Set apart for a particular use or person; hence, belonging peculiarly; suitable; fit; befitting; proper.
{{smaller block|It might be thought to be rather a matter of dignity than any matter of diffidence ''appropriate'' to his own case.{{right|''Bacon.''}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>More ''appropriate'' instances abound.</poem>{{right|''Browning'', Ring and Book, II. 124.}}}}

readings.c2s2:
¶

{{smaller block|A warlike, a refined, an industrial society, each evokes and requires its specific qualities and produces its ''appropriate'' type.{{right|''Lecky'', Europ. Morals, I. 165.}}}}
='''Syn.''' Apt, becoming, in keeping, felicitous.

'''II.†''' ''n.'' Peculiar characteristic; attribute; proper function; property.
{{smaller block|The Bible’s ''appropriate'' being . . . to enlighten the eyes and make wise the simple.{{right|''Boyle'', Style of Holy Scripture, p. 44.}}}}

'''appropriately''' (a̤-prō′pri-āt-li), ''adv.'' In an appropriate or proper manner; fittingly; suitably.

'''appropriateness''' (a̤-prō′pri-āt-nes), ''n.'' The quality of being appropriate or suitable; applicability.
{{smaller block|A hunting-box, a park-lodge, may have a forest grace and the beauty of ''appropriateness.'' ''De Quincey'', Style, i.}}

'''appropriation''' (a̤-prō-pri-ā′shọn), ''n.'' [= F. ''appropriation'', < LL. ''appropriatio(n-)'', < ''appropriare:'' see ''appropriate.''] '''1.''' The act of appropriating, setting apart, or assigning to a particular use or person in exclusion of all others; application to a special use or purpose; specifically, an act of a legislature authorizing money to be paid from the treasury for a special use.—'''2.''' Anything appropriated or set apart for a special purpose, as money.
{{smaller block|The specific ''appropriations'' made by Congress for the mints and assay offices of the United States during the fiscal year ended June 30, 1886, amounted to $1,169,350.{{right|''Report of Sec. of the Treasury'', 1886, I. 157.}}}}
'''3†.''' Acquisition; addition.
{{smaller block|He doth nothing but talk of his horse; and he makes it a great ''appropriation'' to his own good parts that he can shoe him himself.{{right|''Shak.'', M. of V., i. 2.}}}}

