'''2.''' To show; prove to be; demonstrate.
{{smaller block|In all things ye have ''approved'' yourselves to be clear in this matter.{{right|2 Cor. vii. 11.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>’Tis an old lesson; Time ''approves'' it true.</poem>{{right|''Byron'', Childe Harold, ii. 35.}}}}
'''3.''' To sanction officially; ratify authoritatively: as, the decision of the court martial was ''approved''.
{{smaller block|<poem>And by thy coming certainly ''approve''
The pledge of peace. ''Ford'', Honour Triumphant.</poem>}}
'''4.''' To pronounce good; think or judge well of; admit the propriety or excellence of; be pleased with; commend: as, on trial the goods were ''approved;'' to ''approve'' the policy of the administration.
{{smaller block|Yet their posterity ''approve'' their sayings.{{right|Ps. xlix. 13.}}}}
{{smaller block|The deed which closed the mortal course of these sovereigns, I shall neither ''approve'' nor condemn.{{right|''Jefferson'', Autobiog., p. 82.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>She wore the colours I ''approved''.</poem>{{right|''Tennyson'', The Letters.}}}}
'''5.''' To manifest as worthy of approval; commend: used reflexively.
{{smaller block|The miracles of Christianity, so far from shocking me, ''approve'' themselves at once to my intellect and my heart.{{right|''Channing'', Perfect Life, p. 248.}}}}
'''6.''' To put to the test; prove by trial; try.
{{smaller block|<poem>Nay, task me to my word; ''approve'' me, lord.</poem>{{right|''Shak.'', 1 Hen. IV., iv. 1.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>A hundred knights with Palamon there came,
''Approv’d'' in fight, and men of mighty name.</poem>{{right|''Dryden'', Pal. and Arc., l. 1299.}}}}
Hence—'''7†.''' To convict upon trial or by proof.

